#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124247 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3991a54-342b-5cd0-87e2-606146240555" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Two Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "526b792b-9952-5dae-bcd6-15564c0e013f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3131" :question/problem "Find $\\nabla f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,\\pi)$ given that $f(x,y) = (x - y)\\cos y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((x-y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(x-y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the gradient at the specified point, we substitute $(x,y)= (1,\\pi)$ into the above, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(1,\\pi ) & ={f}_{x}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(1,\\pi )\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\text{ }i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +(1-\\pi )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124248 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "bfade25e-065a-5c66-a554-9ee4e9559a55" :question/math-academy-id "e-3132" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\nabla g(x,y,z)$   at $(2,-1,3)$ if $g(x,y,z) = x^2 + xyz.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =2x+yz \\\\ {g}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xz \\\\  & =xz \\\\ {g}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+xyz) \\\\  & =0+xy \\\\  & =xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y,z) & ={g}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{g}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{g}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(2x+yz)\\text{ }i+(xz)\\text{ }j+(xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇g(2,-1,3) & =(2(2)+(-1)(3))\\text{ }i+2(3)\\text{ }j+2(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =1\\text{ }i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k \\\\  & =i+6\\text{ }j-2\\text{ }k.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124249 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124445 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8d807a6-7896-5045-a14a-908b58831340" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Points Where a Gradient Vanishes"}] :question/id #uuid "b2549d7b-07c2-5f2f-89a3-2cccecf2ba22" :question/math-academy-id "e-3133" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)= (y-4)\\ln (x-2),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y-4}{x-2} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y-4)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y-4}{x-2} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x-2) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\  \\mathbf i + 0\\ \\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-4}{x-2}=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-2)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y-4=0,$ which means $y=4.$  From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x-2)=0,$ which means that $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf  0$ at the point $(3,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124251 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/id #uuid "67e9bcb5-4bf2-56b8-8d82-95355841eaaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-3150" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y) g(x,y)$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\sqrt{xy},\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\nabla [fg] = g \\, \\nabla\\! f  + f \\, \\nabla\\! g.\n$$\n\nUsing the product rule shown above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+xy(\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{xy}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+\\frac{\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{xy}}{2}(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe2c7ce2-f053-57af-9f4d-54bd3a41648a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient of a Function of Three Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35a99596-2194-56d8-b34a-8cf33e24d509" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{xyz}(x\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }j+z\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321558} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa3bf93-c16d-5b28-921e-41079fe50f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{xyz}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322014} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfd3cdb-b6de-5b57-8bc2-55f6cfdc0358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322622} {:answer/id #uuid "ce59c82e-5d51-5485-be48-7fb4d8c385b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(x\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }j+z\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322758} {:answer/id #uuid "def79f1e-967d-536d-901e-8b6506e4e5d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{xyz}(\\frac{1}{yz}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{xz}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{xz}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdfd3cdb-b6de-5b57-8bc2-55f6cfdc0358" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322622} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1682cef-8ea8-5e2a-8b48-c4bffce5ce2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4903eb08-1095-52c0-bd3a-a8d7c39455f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-36918" :question/problem "Given $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{xyz}$, find $∇f(x,y,z).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\sqrt{xyz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{xyz}}\\cdot (xy) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2z}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{xyz}}{2}(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335800 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124447 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67cfec73-130c-5b68-a2e0-5f12837bfc81" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Gradient Using the Properties of the Gradient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10ef7585-f490-52ce-84d4-c14afbd71f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+2)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+2)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333143} {:answer/id #uuid "21b12eb3-67b9-5f76-80f9-150884fc0d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333290} {:answer/id #uuid "3ea7c594-0a56-5e0b-a1fd-7fd5413db99e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i-y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333580} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a4ec80-da82-5689-8ecb-e79e98294b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335176} {:answer/id #uuid "fe69f0a9-435b-5c17-9a63-9c54c8b0d0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335228}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21b12eb3-67b9-5f76-80f9-150884fc0d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333290} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8f1d221-bfab-57b4-bd66-7eea20107505" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "838d032d-5600-5cb9-9af4-36cc70864ee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-65131" :question/problem "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =xy,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy),\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))(y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j)+xy(\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+y\\text{ }i+x\\text{ }j \\\\  & =[y\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }i+y\\text{ }i]+[x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)\\text{ }j+x\\text{ }j] \\\\  & =y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }i+x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)+1)\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
